  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nMonsoon thunderstorms put on a show over the mountains Monday \nafternoon, particularly in Riverside and San Diego Counties. A \nsimilar set up today will likely allow for another round of \nthunderstorms over the mountains this afternoon. The ridge over the \nFour Corners region and the weak trough to the northwest remain \nlocked in place. Another surge of monsoon moisture should provide \nPWATs around 1.5\" or slightly higher, similar to what was seen \nyesterday. The latest CAM guidance suggests rainfall rates of up\nto 1\" per hour once again with the highest rainfall rates over San\nDiego and Riverside Counties where the moisture is best. Further\nnorth, rainfall rates of 0.5-0.75\" remain possible over the\neastern San Bernardino County mountains and locally into the High\nDesert. Lower PWATs further west should limit storm activity \ncloser to the LA County line.\n\nThe only notable difference in today's set up is that the mean \nflow is expected to weaken slightly and shift more out of the \nsouth by this afternoon. With the flow more parallel to ridge \nlines in San Diego and Riverside Counties, storms may be able to \nmaintain themselves for longer and there may even be a few cases \nof training storms. This theoretically increases the risk of flash\nflooding today, but the outcome will depend on exactly where \nconvection eventually sets up. Regardless, rainfall rates up to 1\"\nper hour will be enough for a marginal risk of flash flooding in \nitself. Additionally, the southerly orientation of flow will all\nbut eliminate any chance of a storm drifting into the eastern\nvalleys, but a stray storm in the low deserts remains possible. As\nalways with any thunderstorm, frequent lightning, gusty winds, \nand small hail will be possible. Ensure you are prepared to take \nshelter in a sturdy and enclosed structure should you hear \nthunder.\n\nBy Wednesday, the ridge over the Four Corners weakens some and \nretreats eastward and the trough to the west deepens. Increasing \ntrough influence will turn the flow out of the southwest, which \nwill cut off the monsoon moisture. Exactly when this occurs will \ndetermine whether or not a storm or two can develop over the \nmountains on Wednesday. If anything develops, rainfall rates \nshould be lower than today with the most likely location for \nconvection being near Big Bear Lake. \n\nAs the trough continues to deepen just offshore, the cooling \ntrend will become more pronounced from Wednesday onward. Many \nareas will see their coolest temperatures since early July from \nWednesday through the weekend with high temperatures ranging 3 to \n12 degrees below normal along with slightly below normal low \ntemperatures. Also, the marine layer is expected to deepen as\nupper heights fall through the week. Marine layer clouds may fill\nmost of the coastal basin Thu-Fri mornings. Increased onshore \nflow will create breezy conditions for the mountains and deserts \nwith the strongest winds expected Wednesday and Thursday \nafternoons and evenings.\n\nFrom this weekend into early next week, model guidance deals with\na battle between the rebuilding ridge to the east and a\nreinforcing trough diving out of the northwest. The timing,\nstrength, and locations of these features creates minor differences\nin model solutions heading into next week. However, the ensemble\nmean suggests slightly below normal temperatures and little to no\nchance for a return of the monsoon through at least mid-next week.",
